  <section class="legal-hero"><span class="legal-kicker">Legal</span><h1>Privacy Policy</h1><p class="legal-meta"><strong>Effective:</strong> August 24, 2026 · <strong>Last updated:</strong> August 24, 2026</p><p class="legal-summary">Operly is an AI-powered operating platform developed and maintained by Dragonzpyder Industries. This policy explains how information is collected, used, stored, disclosed, and protected across Operly accounts, Personal AI, workspaces, Studio, connectors, plugins, APIs, agents, automations, and related services.</p></section>
  <article class="legal-content">
    <h2>1. Information we collect</h2>
    <h3>Account and profile information</h3><p>We may collect information you provide when creating or managing an account, including your name, email address, authentication information, profile information, workspace memberships, roles, and preferences. Passwords are processed as authentication credentials and stored using one-way password hashing rather than in plaintext.</p>
    <h3>Workspace and business information</h3><p>When you create or use a workspace, we may process workspace names, memberships, roles, business records, CRM information, operations data, tasks, approvals, configuration, generated applications, Studio projects, files, and other information you or authorized workspace members provide.</p>
    <p class="legal-callout"><strong>Personal and workspace scopes are separate authorization boundaries.</strong> A workspace does not automatically receive access to a user's private Personal AI conversation or personal connector data merely because that user belongs to the workspace.</p>
    <h3>Prompts, conversations, files, and generated content</h3><p>We may process prompts, messages, instructions, uploaded files, generated files, model responses, workflow state, agent plans, tool results, application source code, and other content needed to provide AI and automation features.</p>
    <h3>Connector and integration data</h3><p>If you connect a third-party service, Operly may receive and process information authorized by the permissions you grant. Depending on the connector, this can include account identifiers, profile information, email, calendar events, messages, files, channels, servers, contacts, business profile information, metadata, or other data available through the third-party API.</p><p>Operly uses connector permissions to provide user-requested features, agent capabilities, automation, search, retrieval, and authorized actions. Personal connectors and workspace connectors may be governed by different authorization boundaries. You can revoke a connection through Operly where supported and may also revoke access through the third-party provider.</p>
    <h3>Google user data</h3><p>If you connect a Google account, Operly may access Google user data only within the OAuth scopes you authorize, such as identity information, Gmail data, Google Calendar data, or other supported Google services. Operly uses this data to provide features you request, including searching or summarizing authorized information, creating user-requested context for an AI interaction, or carrying out authorized actions through a Google connector.</p><p><strong>Operly does not sell Google user data and does not use Google Workspace API data for advertising.</strong> Operly does not permit humans to read Google user data except when necessary for security, abuse investigation, support requested by the user, legal compliance, or where the user has expressly authorized access.</p><p>Operly's use and transfer to any other application of information received from Google APIs will adhere to the <strong>Google API Services User Data Policy</strong>, including the <strong>Limited Use requirements</strong>.</p>
    <h3>Technical, security, and usage information</h3><p>We may collect IP address, browser and device information, request metadata, timestamps, session information, security events, API activity, error logs, performance data, model/provider identifiers, token usage, tool invocation metadata, approval events, and audit information to operate, secure, debug, improve, and understand the Services.</p>
    <h3>Cookies and local storage</h3><p>Operly may use cookies, browser storage, and similar technologies for authentication, session continuity, security, user preferences, and necessary product functionality. If non-essential advertising or analytics technologies are introduced, we will provide additional notice or controls where required by law.</p>

    <h2>2. How we use information</h2><p>We may use information to provide and improve the Services; authenticate users; provide Personal AI and workspace functionality; run AI models and agent workflows; execute plugins, connectors, tools, automations, Studio projects, and authorized actions; enforce permissions and approvals; remember context where enabled; create or operate user-requested solutions; prevent fraud and abuse; troubleshoot errors; measure reliability and usage; comply with law; enforce agreements; and send important service or security communications.</p>

    <h2>3. AI models and service providers</h2><p>Operly may route user content to AI model providers and other infrastructure or service providers when required to perform a requested feature. Depending on configuration, these may include third-party models or services or providers accessed using user-supplied credentials.</p><p>We seek to send only information reasonably necessary for the requested operation and to respect the active account, workspace, permission, and connector scope. Third-party provider terms may also apply when you intentionally connect or configure that provider.</p><p>Operly does not claim ownership of your prompts, workspace data, files, or other content merely because you use the Services. We do not use private customer content to train a generally available Operly foundation model unless we provide a separate disclosure and obtain any consent required by law or contract.</p>

    <h2>4. Agent actions, approvals, and tool use</h2><p>Operly can allow AI agents to read authorized information and, when permitted, take actions through plugins and connectors. Actions may include creating or editing records, sending communications, modifying files, scheduling events, publishing content, or operating user-created solutions.</p><p>Operly uses authorization, policy, and approval controls to limit actions according to the active user, workspace, connector, and configured permissions. Certain sensitive or consequential write actions may require explicit approval before execution. No technical control can eliminate every risk, so users should review connector permissions and automation settings appropriate to their use case.</p>

    <h2>5. Personal and workspace boundaries</h2><ul><li><strong>Personal scope:</strong> belongs to the individual account and may include private conversations, personal preferences, and personal connectors.</li><li><strong>Workspace scope:</strong> belongs to the relevant workspace and is available according to workspace roles and permissions.</li></ul><p>A user's membership in a workspace does not by itself authorize that workspace to read private Personal AI conversation or personal connector content. A Personal AI may be allowed to reason across workspaces the user is authorized to access when the user requests or configures that behavior.</p>

    <h2>6. How we share information</h2><p>We may disclose information to service providers that process information for us; third-party services you deliberately connect or instruct Operly to use; workspace members according to permissions and your actions; parties involved in a corporate transaction subject to appropriate protections; authorities when required by law; or when reasonably necessary to protect users, Operly, third parties, or the public from fraud, abuse, security threats, or unlawful activity.</p><p>We do not sell personal information for money. If our practices change in a way that constitutes a “sale,” “sharing,” or targeted advertising under applicable privacy law, we will provide required notices and choices.</p>

    <h2>7. Data retention</h2><p>We retain information for as long as reasonably necessary to provide the Services, maintain account and workspace continuity, satisfy security and audit needs, resolve disputes, enforce agreements, and comply with law. Retention periods vary by data type and feature. We may retain limited backup, fraud-prevention, audit, or legal-compliance records after deletion where necessary and permitted by law.</p>

    <h2>8. Security</h2><p>We use administrative, technical, and organizational safeguards designed to protect information, including access controls, authorization boundaries, secure session controls, credential protection, auditability, and security monitoring appropriate to the Services. No service can guarantee absolute security. Users are responsible for protecting credentials, reviewing workspace membership, limiting connector scopes, and promptly reporting suspected unauthorized access.</p>

    <h2>9. Your choices and rights</h2><p>Depending on your location and applicable law, you may have rights to request access to, correction of, deletion of, or a copy of personal information, or to object to or restrict certain processing. You may also be able to update account information, manage workspace membership, disconnect connectors, revoke OAuth access with a provider, delete supported content, or request account deletion. We may need to verify your identity before completing certain requests.</p>

    <h2>10. U.S. state privacy disclosures</h2><p>Residents of certain U.S. states may have additional rights regarding categories of personal information collected, sources, purposes, recipients, access, correction, deletion, portability, and certain forms of sale, sharing, or targeted advertising. Operly does not currently sell personal information for money and does not use Google Workspace API data for advertising. Where a state law applies, we will honor verified requests as required.</p>

    <h2>11. International users</h2><p>Operly may process information in the United States and other countries where our service providers operate. These locations may have data-protection laws different from those in your country. Where required, we will use lawful mechanisms for international transfers.</p>

    <h2>12. Children's privacy</h2><p>The Services are intended for adults and organizations and are not directed to children under 13. We do not knowingly collect personal information from children under 13 through the Services.</p>

    <h2>13. Third-party services</h2><p>Operly may link to or integrate with third-party products. Their privacy practices are governed by their own policies. Connecting a third-party service does not make Operly responsible for that provider's independent practices.</p>

    <h2>14. Changes to this policy</h2><p>We may update this Privacy Policy as Operly evolves. If we make material changes, we will update the date above and provide additional notice when required by law.</p>

    <h2>15. Contact</h2><p>For privacy questions or requests, use the support or contact channels made available through Operly or <a href="https://dragonzpyder.xyz/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dragonzpyder Industries</a>.</p>
